From e0684408035dbbcffeef665251d93102d56e6d7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:40:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer, DVDInputStreamBluray: the audio or subtitle
 stream chosen on a Blu-ray is the one that plays, and the disc is told

On a Blu-ray, IsBetterStream() opens the stream that the disc chose (m_dvd,
the PID of libbluray's PSR1 and PSR2) at each packet of it. A stream the
user chose in Kodi was opened, and at the next packet of the disc's stream
the player went back to that one: choosing another audio or subtitle stream
did nothing, with the disc's menus and without them. The disc never heard of
the choice either, so its program and the play items after it kept the old
one.

Now the stream the player chooses is the one IsBetterStream() keeps, and it
is told to the disc as a player's own menu tells it (bd_select_stream(), by
its number in the stream number table of the play item being played; a
stream the table does not list has no number and is not told). libbluray
answers with its own events, which give the player the stream it already
plays. While an event that changes the play item waits (held for
NextStream(), or left for the thread that reads), the choice is told as
soon as that play item is known, after the disc's own choice made before it
(the one given again with the play item): the last choice stays, and a
choice of the disc's made later is followed as before.

With a playlist played directly, the streams that OpenDefaultStreams()
opens by Kodi's preferences (and the user's choice for this file) are the
ones told: libbluray's choice by language alone no longer takes their place
at the next packet, and a subtitle stream that Kodi leaves closed is not
opened by itself. Turning the subtitles on or off tells the disc too (the
display flag of PSR2), with the stream that plays.

Each choice told is said in the log, with the number it was told by, or why
the disc was not told.

The idea of telling libbluray the stream chosen in Kodi is matthane's and
pannal's (CoreELEC, "keep stream picks in Blu-ray navigation mode", and
"switching audio or subtitle tracks in Blu-ray menu mode works now").
